Pins that a bash case statement's pattern-closing parens (1) and *)) are not mistaken for unmatched subshell-grouping parens -- an unmatched ) at top level is legal bash only as a case pattern terminator, so the tokenizer must treat a stray top-level ) as a plain separator rather than raising an unterminated-parenthesis parse error (an unmatched ( still must error, since there is no legal bash construct that opens a paren without a matching close at top level).
